\DOC distinctness

\TYPE {distinctness : string -> thm}

\SYNOPSIS
Produce distinctness theorem for an inductive type.

\DESCRIBE
A call {distinctness "ty"} where {"ty"} is the name of a recursive type defined
with {define_type}, returns a ``distinctness'' theorem asserting that elements
constructed by different type constructors are always different. The effect is
exactly the same as if {prove_constructors_distinct} were applied to the
recursion theorem produced by {define_type}, and the documentation for
{prove_constructors_distinct} gives a lengthier discussion. The theorem is
proved the first time it is requested for a type, and then saved (see
{distinctness_store}).

\FAILURE
Fails if {ty} is not the name of a recursive type, or if the type has only one
constructor.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # distinctness "num";;
  val it : thm = |- !n'. ~(0 = SUC n')

  # distinctness "list";;
  val it : thm = |- !a0' a1'. ~([] = CONS a0' a1')
}

\COMMENTS
The theorem has a conjunct for each pair of constructors, so it can be large
for types with many constructors. The conversion {CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV}
proves the distinctness of particular pairs of constructors without it.

\SEEALSO
cases, CONSTRUCTORS_DISTINCT_CONV, define_type, distinctness_store,
injectivity, prove_constructors_distinct.

\ENDDOC
